            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house is reasonably air tight, however the majority of the "annoying" air leakage is associated with the older windows (that you are planning to replace) and that ventilation system located in the laundry room.



Based on the fresh air requirements of this home, it appears your house may be under-ventilated and will become tighter in conjunction with the windows replacement. I think it would be a good idea for you to have a qualified mechanical &amp; ventilation design contractor look at the existing system to see if it could be replaced with a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) system (see the HRV section of my report.



Having a properly designed ventilation system means that you do not have to be concerned about making your house even tighter by replacing windows and upgrading the boiler. Completing these upgrades may just decrease the current air leakage by a minimum 10%. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 79&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. You could then apply these savings &amp; rebate $$ towards the new ventilation system.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <windows>

I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of the older original wood casement windows to ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows.





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all of the windows with ENERGY STAR® qualified units rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 221&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV)



During my assessment, your house appears to be slightly under ventilated (please read the section of this report on Air Sealing"). Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



I recommend that you have that older ventilation unit (in the laundry room) reviewed for replacement with an ENERGY STAR® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an ENERGY STAR® rated HRV, there is an estimated  &lt;b&gt; $ 113&lt;/b&gt; increase per year in energy costs, and an associated increase in annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. You will notice in my air sealing section, the estimated savings from reduced air leakage (windows &amp; boiler upgrade) will help offset most of the increased cost and energy of the new HRV, as you are essentially eliminating uncontrolled (and uncomfortable) air leakage wiith a controlled and well designed HRV. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

The domestic hot water for your house is currently supplied by a dedicated loop from the oil boiler, incorporating a 41 USG Amtrol Boilermate storage tank. The hot water heating component is estimated to operate a 0.56 Efficiency Factor (EF) - approximately the annual percentage of heat that is delivered to the hot water verses what percentage goes up the chimney or is lost through short cycling inefficiencies of the boiler (especially during the summer months).



Please look at my upgrade recommendation to the current oil boiler. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE) - the average efficiency over the whole heating season, not just the peak efficiency. Using this boiler to also heat your domestic hot water (still using the Amtrol Boilermate storage tank) will increase the hot water efficiency to 0.79 EF.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

The estimated annual savings have been incorporated into the savings of the boiler upgrade, so please have a look at that section of my report.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil boiler is an older Weil McLain unit that is estimated to be about 83% Steady State Efficiency (SSE) - which is a peak efficiency rating. This boiler is also used to heat the domestic hot water, including the 41 USG Amtrol Boilermate storage tank. The hot water heating component is estimated to operate a 0.56 Efficiency Factor (EF) - approximately the annual percentage of heat that is delivered to the hot water verses what percentage goes up the chimney or is lost through short cycling inefficiencies of the boiler (especially during the summer months). The current boiler and hot water units are in an enclosed mechanical room in the lower level of the house and the boiler vents up a 6" chimney. The mechanical room also has an open 6" combustion air supply pipe that brings combustion air in for the boiler to use.



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE) - the average efficiency over the whole heating season, not just the peak efficiency. Using this boiler to also heat your domestic hot water (still using the Amtrol Boilermate storage tank) will increase the hot water efficiency to 0.79 EF. The new boiler is a direct vent unit, thereby eliminating the need for the current 6" chimney &amp; the 6" open combustion air pipe.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 989&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs ($804 for heating &amp; $185 for hot water), decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;22.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;., and you would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for the new boiler.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------









</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the rear SE facing roof of your house, flush mounted on the roof, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save about $ 906&lt;/b&gt; per year in electricity costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 5,000 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system: 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $5,000 in a diesel electricity community. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has an older toilet and you should consider replacing it with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing this toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



Your home currently has the following ventilation:



1. The kitchen range hood fan vents to the outside, however it is older &amp; weak.

2. The bathroom fan vents to the outside, is quite old and does not operate very well.

3. There is a dryer that vents to the outside.

4. There is a "make-up" ventilation system in the lower level laundry room that is supposed to introduce fresh air into the house, including a boiler pre-heat coil to temper the incoming air from outside.



I would recommend you consider upgrading both the range hood fan and the bathroom fans to ENERGY STAR rated units, that operate at a low sone (noise) level.



For the make-up ventilation system, it looks like it is supposed to operate automatically either on demand when another exhaust fan is on, or perhaps it is interconnected to the humidistat upstairs - turning on both the range hood fan &amp; the fresh air ventilation automatically whenever there is too much humidity in the house.



As you will see in the Ventilation System section of my report, I suggest you have the system evaluated by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. They will be able to determine if it can be replaced with an ENERGY STAR® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood, the bathroom fan, and the dryer indicated a pressure drop of 5.6 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current boiler vents up a 6" chimney, however it is located in a separate &amp; sealed mechanical room, so the depressurization inside the main house should not affect the current boiler. If you upgrade the boiler to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency, direct-vent, condensing propane boiler, the current chimney can be eliminated .......... removing all possibility of backdrafting combustion gases into your home.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Fort Simpson&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Simpson:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh up to the TPSP threshold, $0.70 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.07 per litre 

Propane - $0.60 per litre

Wood - $367.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.35 per bag, $393.75 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



